# Command: MR (Read request frame + Answer frame)
# Radio: Kenwood TS-480HX / TS-480SAT
# Title (cover, verbatim): "PC CONTROL COMMAND REFERENCE FOR THE TS-480HX/ SAT
#   TRANSCEIVER"
# Printed revision: NO revision number is printed anywhere on the cover or the pages
#   read.  The cover prints "KENWOOD CORPORATION", "(c) 2003/11/28" and the printing
#   code line "09 08 07 06 05 04 03 02 01 00".
# Source page: printed folio "13"  ==  PDF page 14 of 26
# Charts used: the Read chart and the Answer chart of the MR block.  The block ALSO
#   prints a "Set" row whose ruler 1..10 is drawn but whose ten cells are ALL EMPTY -
#   see FINDING F3.
# Derivation date: 05/09/2026
# NO code, no generator, no other file and no other document was consulted.
#
# COUNTED FRAME LENGTHS (two independent counts of the printed numbered ruler, then a
# third look at 600 dpi at the terminator cell of the Read row):
#   MR Set    = 0 positions filled (ruler printed 1..10, every cell empty)
#   MR Read   = 7 positions
#   MR Answer = 50 positions  (five ruler rows: 1-10, 11-20, 21-30, 31-40, 41-50)
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# READ CHART, position by position:
#   1  M
#   2  R
#   3  P1
#   4  P2
#   5  P3
#   6  P3
#   7  ;        <-- a SEMICOLON.  Verified at 600 dpi.  The TS-590 book prints a
#                   COLON in the very same cell of its MR Read chart (FINDING F1).
#   8,9,10  empty
#
# ANSWER CHART, position by position:
#   1  M          2  R
#   3  P1
#   4  P2         5-6  P3
#   7-17  P4      (11 positions)
#   18  P5
#   19  P6
#   20  P7
#   21-22  P8
#   23-24  P9
#   25-27  P10
#   28  P11
#   29  P12
#   30-38  P13   (9 positions)
#   39-40  P14
#   41  P15
#   42-49  P16   (8 positions)
#   50  ;
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# LEGEND TEXT, VERBATIM (right-hand column of the MR block, headed "Parameters:";
# this book prints NO "[model] common" banner):
#
# P1 legend (the 0/1 flag and its notes) VERBATIM:
#   P1
#     0: RX frequency, 1: TX frequency
#   and, at the foot of the same column:
#     Memory channel 90 ~ 99:
#     P1=0 (start frequency), P1=1 (end frequency)
#
#   P2
#     Always 0 for the TS-480.
#   P3
#     00 ~ 99: Memory channel number
#   P4
#     Frequency in Hz (11-digit).
#
# Mode legend VERBATIM (MR prints only a cross-reference, not the table):
#   P5
#     Mode.  Refer to the MD command.
#   -- the MD table itself (folio 12 / PDF p.13) reads VERBATIM:
#      "0: No mode (Not used for the TS-480) / 1: LSB / 2: USB / 3: CW / 4: FM /
#       5: AM / 6: FSK / 7: CWR (CW Reverse) / 8: Tune (Not used for the TS-480) /
#       9: FSR (FSK Reverse)"
#
# Tone legends VERBATIM:
#   P7
#     0: OFF, 1: TONE, 2: CTCSS
#   P8
#     Tone Number.  Refer to page 35.
#   P9
#     CTCSS tone number.  Refer to the CN command.
#   INDEX -> Hz TABLES: BOTH ARE ABSENT from this book.  No tone or CTCSS
#     index-to-frequency table is printed anywhere in it.  The two commands the
#     legends point at print only ranges and an outward cross-reference:
#       TN (folio 21 / PDF p.22) VERBATIM: "P1 / 00 ~ 42" and "Refer to page 32 of the
#         TS-480 instruction manual for the Tone numbers and frequencies."
#       CN (folio 5 / PDF p.6) VERBATIM: "P1 / 00 ~ 41" and "Refer to page 33 of the
#         TS-480 instruction manual for the CTCSS tone numbers and frequencies."
#     MR's own P8 line points at "page 35", which is not a page of this 26-page
#     document (see FINDING F4) - and MW's P8 line in the same book points at the TN
#     command instead (see FINDING F6).
#
# EVERY POSITION THE CHART PRINTS AS FIXED / "Always" (by position number), with the
# legend text VERBATIM.  NOTE how many more there are than in the TS-590 book:
#   position  4         (P2)   -- "P2 / Always 0 for the TS-480."
#   positions 25,26,27  (P10)  -- "P10 / Always 000 for the TS-480."
#   position  28        (P11)  -- "P11 / Always 0 for the TS-480."
#   position  29        (P12)  -- "P12 / Always 0 for the TS-480."
#   positions 30..38    (P13)  -- "P13 / Always 000000000 for the TS-480."
#   position  41        (P15)  -- "P15 / Always 0 for the TS-480."
#   ("Always" is the only such wording used; the book prints no "not used" note in
#    the MR block.  The wording "Not used for the TS-480" DOES appear, but only in the
#    MD mode table quoted above, for mode values 0 and 8.)
#
# The remaining variable legends VERBATIM:
#   P6
#     Lockout status.  0: Lockout OFF, 1: Lockout ON
#   P14
#     Step size.  Refer to the ST command.
#   P16
#     Memory name.  A maximum of 8 characters.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# BYTES WHOSE VALUE THE MANUAL DOES NOT DETERMINE  -> INHERITED-ASSUMED
#
#   [A1] positions 7..17 (P4) - only "Frequency in Hz (11-digit)." is documented; the
#        frequency VALUE in each vector is ASSUMED.  (The AS command's P3 legend in
#        the same book adds "Unused digit(s) must be 0.", which is what the leading
#        zeros below follow.)
#   [A2] positions 42..49 (P16) when the name is SHORTER than 8 characters.
#        WHAT THE BOOK SAYS ABOUT PADDING: for MR it says only "P16 / Memory name.  A
#        maximum of 8 characters."  It says NOTHING about justification or a pad
#        character.  The nearest general statement in the book is the folio-2 note
#        "If a particular parameter is not applicable to this transceiver, the
#        parameter digits should be filled using any character except the ASCII
#        control codes (00 to 1Fh) and the terminator (;)." - which is about
#        inapplicable parameters, not about short names.  ASSUMED: the name is
#        left-justified and padded on the right with ASCII space (0x20) to 8.
#   [A3] positions 21..22 (P8) and 23..24 (P9) when the corresponding tone system is
#        OFF.  No don't-care value is printed.  ASSUMED: "00".
#   [A4] positions 39..40 (P14) VALUES.  The ST legend (folio 20 / PDF p.21) reads
#        VERBATIM "SSB/ CW/ FSK mode: 00 ~ 04 / 00: 0.5 kHz, 01: 1 kHz, 02: 2.5 kHz,
#        03: 5 kHz, 04: 10 kHz / AM/ FM mode: 00 ~ 09 / 00: 5 kHz, 01: 6.25 kHz,
#        02: 10 kHz, 03: 12.5 kHz, / 04: 15 kHz, 05: 20 kHz, 06: 25 kHz, 07: 30 kHz,
#        / 08: 50 kHz, 09: 100 kHz" - so the width 2 is documented but the value in
#        each vector is ASSUMED.
#   [A5] The channel numbers, mode digits and name strings are ASSUMED illustrative
#        values; only their field widths and legends are documented.
#
# Hardware status: UNVERIFIED for every vector in this file.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# VECTORS  (name <TAB> frame bytes verbatim, terminator included; a space inside a
# frame is a real space)
#
# --- Read request frames (7 positions; P2 is the fixed "0") ---
mr480_read_ch03_rx	MR0003;
mr480_read_ch04_rx	MR0004;
mr480_read_ch12_tx	MR1012;
mr480_read_ch90_start_freq	MR0090;
mr480_read_ch90_end_freq	MR1090;
#
# --- Answer frames (50 positions) ---
# A: ordinary simplex channel (P1=0 RX frequency, tone OFF), 8-character name
mr480_answer_ch03_rx_name8	MR000300014250000200000000000000000000000DXCLUSTR;
# B: channel whose name is SHORTER than the field (3 characters + 5 assumed spaces, A2)
mr480_answer_ch04_rx_name3	MR000400007150000100000000000000000000000NET     ;
# C: tone state other than OFF (P7=1 TONE) and the non-simplex indication this book
#    allows (P1=1, the TX frequency); P14=02 (10 kHz AM/FM step)
mr480_answer_ch12_tx_tone_on	MR101200051500000401080000000000000000020REPEATER;
